        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES: <BR>
Interact is a technology demonstration experiment testing the feasibility of controlling a robotics device on the ground from an orbiting spacecraft.   It is known that microgravity adapted crewmembers handle many tools differently than on the ground, and it is therefore assumed that this also effects how astronauts interface with remote control devices such as joystick-like units, particularly when these devices are force sensitive.  A series of three experiments were designed by the ESA Robotics Laboratory to investigate these changes in haptic feedback control.  Interact represents the third and final investigation in that series, the first two experiments were Haptics-1 and Haptics-2.  
<P>
Interact consists of two different experiment protocols: <p>
Interact-1 aims to quantify the performance gains and/or losses of various control techniques using real-time vs delayed communication, and Interact-2 is the validation of a remote execution of a robotic operation on Earth being controlled from space.
<P>
During the Interact-1 testing protocol, crewmembers are asked to perform three different tasks with the Kentaur-Robot (located at ESTEC) via remote control.   The crew interface responses (e.g. applied force, reaction threshold) are then recorded and the crewmember is asked to fill out a short questionnaire afterwards to provide additional feedback.   This session is conducted twice in orbit, one under almost real-time conditions and the other session with a communications delay of several seconds. In addition to the Interact-1 Protocol, one of the participating crewmembers will be asked to perform the Interact-2 protocol, during which the Kentaur-Robot is tasked to drive to the location of a peg, retrieve the peg with the robot’s human-like hands, and drive to a board and insert the peg into a hole on that board.  

<P>

APPROACH: <br>
Preflight crewmembers perform Interact-1 tasks under real-time and delayed communications conditions.  In flight crewmembers are asked to perform the Interact-1 protocol once after 90 days in flight in both communication settings while being adapted to microgravity and to perform the Interact-2 protocol.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
Results for this investigation are unavailable. This is an international experiment. NASA does not currently have an agreement with international space partners to archive their data in the LSDA.
        </div>

